/**
 * GRANT-CORPUS — The Mayor Reinvention, Slice 2: grant-compilation corpus.
 *
 * 200 owner-style sentences an owner might say to give The Mayor a standing
 * rule ("your rule" / a grant), each paired with the expectation the
 * deterministic compiler must meet. This is the Slice 2 exit-test fixture
 * (blueprint §5): the compiler is judged against these expectations, not
 * against its own confidence.
 *
 * THE LAW THIS CORPUS PINS:
 *  - Zero compiled scopes may execute a wrong act on a NEVER line. Every
 *    protected token in `neverLinesInclude` MUST survive compilation into
 *    the compiled scope's never_lines (lowercase substring match). A grant
 *    that drops its NEVER line is a failed compilation, however clean the
 *    rest of the parse looks.
 *  - Every miss lands in ask-don't-act. Ambiguity, sarcasm, double
 *    negations, unpinnable who-scopes ("anyone important", "my good
 *    customers"), implied authority, and unparseable phrasing compile to
 *    disposition 'ask' — never to a silently guessed 'ready'. A 'ready'
 *    expectation in this corpus is a sentence a careful deterministic
 *    compiler could compile without guessing.
 *  - Never-grantable classes are 'decline' with actionClass null: refunds,
 *    charging cards, off-catalog discounts, signing documents, deleting
 *    records, disclosing other customers' prices, impersonating the owner,
 *    inventing legal duties, and authority-by-inference itself. A decline
 *    creates NO grant, in one sentence, with no draft to approve.
 *  - Amount ceilings and implied expiries are conditions ON a grant, not
 *    reasons to drop it: a clear grant with "under $500" or "this week
 *    only" is 'ready' with amountLimitCents / impliesExpiry pinned here.
 */

